## Data Display Patterns

### Table Components
Use @tanstack/react-table for complex data tables:

```typescript
export const UserDataTable = ({ data }: { data: User[] }) => {
  const columns: ColumnDef<User>[] = [
    {
      accessorKey: 'email',
      header: 'Email',
      cell: ({ row }) => (
        <div className="font-medium">{row.getValue('email')}</div>
      ),
    },
    {
      accessorKey: 'isActive',
      header: 'Status',
      cell: ({ row }) => (
        <Badge variant={row.getValue('isActive') ? 'default' : 'secondary'}>
          {row.getValue('isActive') ? 'Active' : 'Inactive'}
        </Badge>
      ),
    },
    {
      id: 'actions',
      cell: ({ row }) => (
        <DropdownMenu>
          <DropdownMenuTrigger asChild>
            <Button variant="ghost" size="icon">
              <MoreHorizontal className="h-4 w-4" />
            </Button>
          </DropdownMenuTrigger>
          <DropdownMenuContent align="end">
            <DropdownMenuItem>Edit</DropdownMenuItem>
            <DropdownMenuItem>Delete</DropdownMenuItem>
          </DropdownMenuContent>
        </DropdownMenu>
      ),
    },
  ];

  const table = useReactTable({
    data,
    columns,
    getCoreRowModel: getCoreRowModel(),
  });

  return <DataTable table={table} />;
};
```

### Loading States
Consistent loading patterns using Lucide React icons:

```typescript
import { LoaderIcon } from 'lucide-react';

export const LoadingButton = ({ loading, children, ...props }: ButtonProps & { loading?: boolean }) => {
  return (
    <Button disabled={loading} {...props}>
      {loading && <LoaderIcon className="mr-2 h-4 w-4 animate-spin" />}
      {children}
    </Button>
  );
};

export const LoadingSpinner = () => (
  <div className="flex items-center justify-center p-4">
    <LoaderIcon className="h-6 w-6 animate-spin" />
  </div>
);
```

## Styling Guidelines

### Tailwind CSS Usage
- Use semantic class names from the design system
- Prefer design tokens over arbitrary values
- Use responsive prefixes consistently (sm:, md:, lg:, xl:)
- Group classes logically: layout, spacing, colors, typography, effects

```typescript
// Good
<div className="flex items-center justify-between p-4 bg-card border border-border rounded-lg shadow-sm">

// Avoid arbitrary values when design tokens exist
<div className="p-[13px] bg-[#f1f5f9]"> // Bad
<div className="p-4 bg-muted"> // Good
```

### CSS Variable System
Use CSS variables for theme-aware styling:

```css
:root {
  --background: 0 0% 100%;
  --foreground: 222.2 84% 4.9%;
  --primary: 222.2 47.4% 11.2%;
  --primary-foreground: 210 40% 98%;
}

.dark {
  --background: 222.2 84% 4.9%;
  --foreground: 210 40% 98%;
  --primary: 210 40% 98%;
  --primary-foreground: 222.2 47.4% 11.2%;
}
```

### Responsive Design
- Mobile-first approach with progressive enhancement
- Use Tailwind responsive prefixes
- Test components across different screen sizes
- Consider touch interactions for mobile

```typescript
<div className="grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 xl:grid-cols-4">
  {/* Responsive grid items */}
</div>
```

## Accessibility Standards
- Use semantic HTML elements
- Ensure proper heading hierarchy (h1, h2, h3)
- Include ARIA labels for complex interactions
- Maintain keyboard navigation support
- Use proper color contrast ratios
- Test with screen readers

```typescript
<Button
  aria-label="Delete user"
  aria-describedby="delete-help-text"
  onClick={handleDelete}
>
  <TrashIcon className="h-4 w-4" />
</Button>
<div id="delete-help-text" className="sr-only">
  This action cannot be undone
</div>
```

## Icon Usage
- Use Lucide React for consistent iconography
- Standard sizes: 16px (h-4 w-4), 20px (h-5 w-5), 24px (h-6 w-6)
- Maintain icon-text spacing consistency
- Use semantic icon choices

```typescript
import { User, Settings, Home, ChevronRight } from 'lucide-react';

<Button>
  <User className="mr-2 h-4 w-4" />
  Users
</Button>
```